<p class="copy">
	Co-H2O/E2 forms the second half of the <?php echo sf_lore_link('fuel-gates', 'fuel gate'); ?>/water gate duopoly of technologies that underpin space travel and colonization in the settled systems. For nearly all purposes, Slim Water is identical to its mundane counterpart. Indeed, its paraphysical effect wasn’t even discovered until the water gate had been commercialized. Liquid Co-H2O/E2 demonstrates hypercompressibility between 12°C and 94°C. Under pressure, porous ice-like structures form throughout the liquid, and the remaining unfrozen molecules wedge into the gaps in their lattice, packing the whole mass down to just 8.35% of its original volume.
</p>
<p class="copy mb-0">
	While its compressibility has been less important than the simple fact that it’s clean water available through a <?php echo sf_lore_link('switch-gates', 'switch gate'); ?>, its paraphysical effect has still allowed for ship designs without the bulky water tanks that were once required. Because Slim Water rapidly expands to its original volume outside that temperature range, most ships are built with overpressure valves venting it either into space or into waterproofed overflow zones. More than a few deck crews have startled awake to find their quarters flooded. With a paraphysical decay half-life of two years, the material will eventually return to its original volume through simple transmutation back into mundane water. Given the impossibility of separating Slim Water and fat water, “water cycling” has become standard in all ship maintenance and repair routines.
</p>
